Title: The Last Fare
Credit: Written by
Author: Project-3
Draft date: October 2026

FADE IN:

INT. ALL-NIGHT DINER - NIGHT

Rain streaks the windows. A neon sign buzzes "open" in backwards red letters.

MARISOL (40s, cab driver, tired eyes, sharp tongue) sits alone at the counter, nursing a CHIPPED MUG of coffee. Her TAXI KEYS lie next to a half-eaten slice of pie.

LOU (60s, cook, apron stained with a decade of grease) leans through the kitchen pass-through, wiping his hands on a DISH TOWEL.

LOU
You've been on that coffee for an hour. It's not getting any warmer.

MARISOL
Neither am I. Slow night.

LOU
Every night's slow after two.

He slides a COFFEE POT across the counter. She refills without looking.

The bell over the door jingles. DEV (20s, rain-soaked, clutching a MANILA ENVELOPE to his chest) stumbles in, scanning the room.

DEV
Is that your cab outside? The yellow one?

MARISOL
Depends who's asking.

DEV
Someone who needs to be at the Harbor Bridge before sunrise.

Marisol checks her WRISTWATCH.

MARISOL
That's forty minutes. Meter starts at the door.

DEV
(pulling out a wad of CASH)
I'll pay double if you don't ask what's in the envelope.

Lou and Marisol exchange a look.

LOU
I'd ask.

MARISOL
(grabbing her keys)
Lou thinks everyone's a criminal.

LOU
Lou's usually right.

EXT. DINER PARKING LOT - NIGHT

Puddles shimmer under a flickering streetlight. Marisol jogs to her TAXI, Dev close behind, shielding the envelope under his jacket.

She stops at the driver's door.

MARISOL
Back seat. And if you drip on the upholstery, that's extra.

Dev climbs in. Marisol glances back at the diner. Lou watches from the window, arms crossed.

INT. TAXI - MOVING - NIGHT

Wipers thump a steady rhythm. The city slides past in smears of light.

Marisol adjusts the REARVIEW MIRROR to watch Dev. He's turning the envelope over and over in his hands.

MARISOL
You're going to wear a hole in that thing.

DEV
Sorry. Nervous habit.

MARISOL
Bridge at dawn. Envelope. Cash. You either proposing or getting blackmailed.

DEV
(beat)
Neither. I'm giving something back.

MARISOL
To who?

DEV
My father. He jumps off that bridge every morning. Swims to the far shore. Sixty-eight years old and he won't miss a day.

Marisol's eyes flick to the mirror.

MARISOL
And what's in the envelope?

DEV
The deed to his house. I sold it out from under him three years ago. Took me this long to buy it back.

A long pause. The wipers thump.

MARISOL
Huh. Not a criminal.

DEV
Not anymore.

INT. TAXI - PARKED - DAWN

The meter reads $47.50. Gray light creeps over the dashboard. Through the windshield: the HARBOR BRIDGE, its cables glowing faintly.

Dev doesn't move.

DEV
What if he doesn't want to see me?

MARISOL
Then you'll have tried. That's more than most.

She shuts off the METER.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
This one's on the house.

EXT. HARBOR BRIDGE - DAWN

Wind off the water. Seagulls wheel overhead.

An OLD MAN (70s, wiry, unbothered by the cold) in a faded swim cap stands at the railing, stretching. His TOWEL hangs over the rail.

Dev approaches slowly, the envelope held out in front of him like an offering.

The old man turns. Recognizes him. Neither speaks.

From the taxi at the end of the bridge, Marisol watches. She picks up her RADIO HANDSET.

MARISOL
(into radio)
Dispatch, this is forty-two. I'm off the clock.

She sets it down, smiles, and pulls away.

FADE OUT.
